---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { site, conversationMailto } from '../../data/site.ts';
import portrait from '../../assets/media/jay-stewart.jpg';
---

<Base
  title="Agent-readiness audit"
  description="The agent-readiness audit, written up as a method: every claim in a CLAUDE.md, rules or memory file verified against the repository, and a CI gate left behind so the lies cannot come back. Run first on my own production repo — findings published."
>
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-content px-5 py-16 sm:px-8 sm:py-20">
    <p class="eyebrow">The method · Agent-readiness audit</p>
    <h1 class="mt-3 text-4xl sm:text-5xl">Your agents obey instructions nobody has checked.</h1>

    <div class="prose mt-10">
      <p>
        Find every sentence in an agent’s instructions that is no longer true, prove each one
        against the actual repository, and leave behind a CI gate so the lies cannot come back.
        The outcome is not a report — it is a permanent check, of which the report is the first
        run. This page is that method, written up: I built it for my own repositories, published
        what it found, and use it as the diagnostic first step when looking at how a team works
        with agents.
      </p>

      <h2>The problem</h2>
      <p>
        Code that lies gets caught by a compiler. Agent context that lies gets{' '}
        <em>executed</em>. Every team that has adopted coding agents has accumulated a layer of
        standing instructions — <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, <code>AGENTS.md</code>, Cursor rules,
        memory files — that agents obey literally, at scale, every session, and that nothing on
        earth verifies. Each sentence was true when it was written. Drift is a property of time,
        not of discipline.
      </p>
      <p>
        I know because mine has been audited. The most disciplined repository I operate — a
        production system carrying real payments, run the way{' '}
        <a href="/work/agent-operated-codebase/">the flagship case study</a> describes — had four
        live instances. Your context layer has never been checked, and mine had four.
      </p>

      <h2>What a finding looks like</h2>
      <p>
        This is the drift report format, populated with the real findings from that audit. Every
        claim is cited to file and line, verified against the code before it is reported, and
        carries a fix rather than a complaint. A suspected finding that turns out to be false is
        labelled as such with its reasoning — never silently dropped. And a clean result is a
        result: findings are never inflated to make the method look necessary.
      </p>
      <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Example drift report" tabindex="0">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Claim in the context layer</th>
              <th scope="col">Verified against the repo</th>
              <th scope="col">Severity</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Environment variables declared as required for two services</td>
              <td>Both services decommissioned months earlier</td>
              <td>High — agents provision for infrastructure that does not exist</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Server configuration referenced as current</td>
              <td>Points at a database that was torn down</td>
              <td>High — a confident agent would try to use it</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Memory file describing the change workflow</td>
              <td>Directly contradicts the project’s stated pull-request policy</td>
              <td>Medium — agents follow whichever version they read first</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Summarised, those rows are a format. <a href="/audit/sample/">Read the excerpt itself</a
        >{' '}
        — four findings at full depth, with the claims quoted from the file, the evidence cited to
        line, the assertions left behind, and the tool's real output on the repository. Including
        the finding no assertion can express, and the one whose check passes while the sentence is
        still wrong.
      </p>

      <h2>What the method produces</h2>
      <p>
        Five artifacts — and the middle three keep running in the repository after the audit is
        over, which is the point.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>The drift report.</strong> Every claim in the context layer that is currently false,
          cited to file and line, severity-rated, each with a concrete fix.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The assertion suite.</strong> A <code>.groundtruth.jsonc</code> committed into the
          repository — the context layer’s own sentences turned into executable checks.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The gate.</strong>{' '}
          <a href="https://groundtruth.sh">groundtruth</a> running on every pull request, failing
          the build when a context claim goes false. Two lines of YAML.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The contradiction pass.</strong> Every context file read against the others for
          direct contradictions, not just individually false statements. No assertion can express
          a claim about two files disagreeing, which is why this is a method and not a download.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The context-economics read.</strong> Which standing rules are load-bearing, which
          are never cited, and what to prune.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Why the evidence is public</h2>
      <p>
        The tool is <a href="https://groundtruth.sh">open source and installable in a minute</a
        >, the practice it came from is written up as <a href="/work/agent-operated-codebase/"
          >a case study with counted figures</a
        >, and the receipts come from a production system with real users and real money moving
        through it. My own best-kept repository went through the audit first and its four
        findings were published rather than the marketing — because a method that has never
        caught its own author is not one worth trusting with anyone else’s repository.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div
      class="mt-14 flex flex-col gap-5 rounded-lg border border-line bg-band p-6 sm:flex-row sm:gap-7 sm:p-8"
    >
      <Image
        src={portrait}
        alt="Jay Stewart"
        width={96}
        densities={[1, 2]}
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
        class="w-20 shrink-0 self-start rounded-lg border border-line bg-surface sm:w-24"
      />

      <div>
        <h2 class="text-xl">Run it yourself, or compare notes</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-muted">
          The tool and the format above are enough to run this on your own repositories today —
          that is a genuinely good outcome and it costs nothing. And if agents write a
          meaningful share of your team’s code, I would like to compare notes either way: what
          drifted, what the gate caught, what no assertion could express.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-5">
          <a href={conversationMailto} class="link-underline text-lg">{site.email}</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
